<script is:inline>

  window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {

    /* ── Crypto Basics Explorer (expanded to include Satoshi) ── */
    const cryptoBasics = {
      hash: {
        title: 'Hash functions',
        body: 'A hash function takes any input and produces a fixed-length output (SHA-256 always produces 64 hex characters). Critical properties: deterministic (same input, same output), one-way (you cannot reverse it to find the input), and avalanche-effect (changing one character in the input completely changes the output). Bitcoin uses SHA-256 twice. Hashes serve as tamper-evident fingerprints: if you know the hash of a 1GB file, you can verify the file has not been altered by recomputing the hash in seconds. Miners search for a nonce such that the block header hash falls below a target value — this is the "work" in proof-of-work.'
      },
      pubkey: {
        title: 'Public/private key cryptography',
        body: 'Asymmetric cryptography uses a mathematically linked key pair. The private key is a secret that must never be exposed. The public key (and the address derived from it) can be shared freely. Ownership becomes the ability to sign: the private key creates proof of authorisation; the public key lets anyone verify it without learning the private key. The maths (elliptic curve cryptography over secp256k1) makes deriving the private key from the public key computationally infeasible — requiring more operations than there are atoms in the observable universe on any foreseeable hardware. Self-custody is powerful precisely because no account reset exists.'
      },
      sig: {
        title: 'Digital signatures',
        body: 'A digital signature proves that a private key approved a specific message, and that it has not been altered. In Bitcoin, when you send a transaction, your wallet signs it with your private key. Miners verify the signature using your public key before including it in a block. This means transactions cannot be forged — only the keyholder can authorise a spend from their address. This is the mechanism that eliminates the need for a trusted intermediary in payment verification: you can broadcast a transaction through entirely hostile networks without trusting the messenger, because the signature travels with it.'
      },
      merkle: {
        title: 'Merkle trees',
        body: 'A Merkle tree is a binary tree of hashes. Each leaf node is a transaction hash; each parent node is the hash of its two children; the root ("Merkle root") summarises all transactions in the block in a single 32-byte hash. This structure makes it computationally cheap to prove that a specific transaction is included in a block (a "Merkle proof") without downloading the entire blockchain. Light clients — mobile wallets — use Merkle proofs to verify transactions without running a full node, which is why Bitcoin can be used on a phone without storing 600GB of blockchain data.'
      },
      satoshi: {
        title: 'Satoshi Nakamoto',
        body: 'The pseudonymous inventor of Bitcoin posted the nine-page whitepaper on 31 October 2008 and mined the genesis block on 3 January 2009, embedding: "Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks." Satoshi communicated via email and forum posts until April 2011, then disappeared entirely. Their true identity remains unknown despite decades of speculation. Estimates suggest they hold around 1.1 million BTC — worth over $100 billion at peak prices — in coins they have never moved. The decision not to sell, ever, is itself part of Bitcoin\'s founding mythology: the creator had no monetary incentive to undermine what they built.'
